**Q2.** What does a *larger* ICC mean here? How does it change (a) effective sample size and (b) how important it is to model site differences?

**KEY (answer):** A larger ICC means more variance is **between sites**, so two observations from the same site are more similar.  
(a) Effective N is smaller than raw N because within-site observations are partly redundant.  
(b) Site differences are substantively important: sites have meaningfully different typical nitrate levels, so modeling site baselines is not just a “statistical fix,” it captures real environmental heterogeneity.

